<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Burgundy</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Burgundian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Burgundy is a historical region of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the name of a powerful late medieval duchy whose lands also included the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a>. Through the marriage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-of-burgundy">Mary of Burgundy</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximilian-i-holy-roman-emperor">Maximilian of Habsburg</a>, the Burgundian inheritance passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>. It formed part of the territories inherited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>, contributing to the foundation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a>'s European <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">dominions</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/burgundy.jpg" alt="Burgundy" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Patricia.fidi · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Bourgogne.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Burgundy is named for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundians">Burgundians</a>, an East Germanic people who moved west beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> in the late Roman period. Gallic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celts">Celts</a>, above all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aedui">Aedui</a>, were the first recorded inhabitants, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> absorbed them after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-alesia">Battle of Alesia</a>. In the 4th century the Burgundians settled in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> and founded a kingdom, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> conquered in 532. In 843 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-verdun">Treaty of Verdun</a> divided the old Burgundian lands, and the north-western part went to the West Frankish Kingdom. From the early 10th century it was organised as the Duchy of Burgundy, and from 1004 the House of Burgundy, a cadet branch of the French royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capetian-dynasty">House of Capet</a>, ruled it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Burgundy was home to some of the most important churches and monasteries of the medieval West. Cluny, founded in 910, influenced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> for centuries, and the first Cistercian abbey was founded at Cîteaux in 1098. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernard-of-clairvaux">Bernard of Clairvaux</a>'s influence helped spread hundreds of Cistercian abbeys across Europe in the following century. During the Hundred Years' War King John II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> gave the duchy to his youngest son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-bold">Philip the Bold</a>, and it became a rival to the crown. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-good">Philip the Good</a> acquired Namur, Hainaut, Brabant and Holland.</p><p>The Valois-Burgundian State held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fiefs</a> from the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a>, and its dukes were among the wealthiest princes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, sometimes called Grand Dukes of the West. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-nancy">Battle of Nancy</a> in 1477 the last duke, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bold">Charles the Bold</a>, was killed. The French crown annexed the duchy, which became a province in 1482, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a> possessions passed to his daughter Mary and her <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> descendants. The division of the Burgundian inheritance marked the beginning of a long French-Habsburg rivalry.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The province of Burgundy lasted until 1790, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> replaced it. Burgundy now means the area of the departments of Côte-d'Or, Saône-et-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yonne-river">Yonne</a> and Nièvre, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dijon">Dijon</a> as its capital. It was an administrative region from 1982 to 2015 and merged with Franche-Comté in 2016. The abbeys of Fontenay and Vézelay are World Heritage Sites, and Vézelay is still a starting point for pilgrimages to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-de-compostela">Santiago de Compostela</a>. Cluny was almost entirely destroyed during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>. The region is also known for Burgundy wine.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Burgundy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Treaty of Verdun (843 CE) formalised a threefold division into West Francia under Charles the Bald, Middle Francia (from the Low Countries through <b>Burgundy</b> to Italy) under Lothair, and East Francia under Louis the German.</blockquote><a href="/empire/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the dukes of <b>Burgundy</b>, Flanders became the core of a rich new state, and its painters, the Flemish Primitives, transformed European art; Jan van Eyck worked in Bruges, and the Ghent Altarpiece was completed in 1432.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-flanders">County of Flanders · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Hainaut passed through several dynasties: the house of Flanders, then the Avesnes, who also held Holland and Zeeland from 1299, then the Wittelsbachs from 1345 and the Valois dukes of <b>Burgundy</b> from 1433, before the Habsburgs inherited it in 1482.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-hainaut">County of Hainaut · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In 893 William the Pious, count of Auvergne, began to style himself duke of the Aquitanians, the first prince to hold the title in this new sense, and in 910 he founded the abbey of Cluny in <b>Burgundy</b>, whose monastic reform would spread across Latin Europe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-aquitaine">Duchy of Aquitaine · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1395 Duke Philip the Bold ordered the Gamay grape uprooted from <b>Burgundy</b>’s vineyards, calling it a ‘very bad and disloyal’ plant, in favour of Pinot noir.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-burgundy">Duchy of Burgundy · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The duchy’s greatest military moment came under René II, whose lands lay across the corridor Charles the Bold of <b>Burgundy</b> needed to link his territories.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-lorraine">Duchy of Lorraine · Military</a></li></ul></section>
